  — a notification only earns its existence when something changes that the user isn't already watching happen.

  Right now the only actor is the person looking at the page. If they edit a task, they know — they just did it.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What is the source of change that a user needs to be told about? → [39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'll put it more plainly, since that's the one answer that decides everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m notifications are only worth building if change arrives from somewhere the user isn't looking. There are two
  very different worlds here, and they share almost no architecture:

  - [1mSingle-user, time-driven[0m — one person, one browser. The only thing that "changes" without them is the clock: a task
    comes due, a reminder fires. Needs localStorage plus a timer and the Notification API. No server, no accounts, no
    auth. Buildable in a day; easy to throw away later.
  - [1mMulti-user, collaboration-driven[0m — shared tasks, someone else reassigns or completes one, you get told. Needs
    accounts, a backend, persisted tasks, a subscription model ("tasks I care about"), an event log, and a delivery
    transport (polling, SSE, or push). Weeks, not a day. Everything about it is expensive to retrofit and expensive to
    remove.

  Picking wrong is not cheap in one direction: building the single-user version when you meant collaboration means
  throwing away the storage layer and starting over. Building the collaboration version when you meant reminders means
  carrying a backend and an auth system you never needed.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m start single-user and time-driven — but only if that actually matches what you have in mind. It's
  the version where "tasks they care about" means "tasks you created and set a date on," and I can design it so the
  storage layer doesn't actively block a later server.

  So, the one question: [1mwhen you pictured a user being notified, who or what had made the change — another person, or [0m
  [1mthe clock?[0m

  I'm not writing any code or a spec until I hear that. With the app being an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m, a guess here would mean
  designing the wrong system in full detail.

